## .tmp/ is the per-worktree scratch + reference-clone area. Per its
## own README (`/.tmp/README.md`) the contents are NEVER committed to
## the chezmoi source tree (only the outer .tmp/ dir is gitignored at the
## project level; chezmoi's walker was re-evaluating it on every
## `chezmoi data` call, paying ~1.5 s to walk the 4.4 GB
## .tmp/references/repository/kilocode/node_modules tree). Excluding
## here keeps chezmoi in sync with the git-level policy.
.tmp

## Post-mortem docs live in the source tree only — reference material
## for maintainers, not files the user needs in ~. Keep the post-mortem
## in the repo so future sessions can read what failed and why.
docs

## shellx documentation lives in the source tree only — not deployed.
## These are reference docs for maintainers, not files the user needs in ~.
dot_shell/helper/.help

## shellx unit tests live in the source tree only — run via
## `python3 dot_shell/helper/test_shellx.py`, not deployed.
**/test_shellx.py
**/__pycache__/**

## Ruff config for the helper scripts lives in the source tree only — keeps
## `~/.shell/helper/pyproject.toml` out of $HOME. Run `ruff check
## dot_shell/helper/` from the repo root to lint.
**/pyproject.toml

## shellx exports are intentionally not auto-restored.
## Use `shellx import <file>` to explicitly restore secrets onto a machine.
.encrypted_data/env_store/shellx*

## OpenCode's background-service credential is generated at runtime by OpenCode
## itself and rotates on its own schedule. It must never enter the source tree:
## it is a plaintext password, and chezmoi would otherwise deploy a stale copy
## that fights the live service. Unmanaged on purpose — chezmoi forget
## ~/.config/opencode/service.json (2026-10-23).
dot_config/opencode/private_service.json

## The embeddings proxy runs under the service manager of the host OS.
## Linux uses a systemd user unit; macOS uses a launchd agent. The proxy
## script itself is shared, so only the unit of the other OS is ignored.
## Ignore the whole directory, not the unit file: chezmoi still creates
## the parent directories of an ignored file, so a file-level ignore
## leaves an empty tree in the home directory of the other OS.
{{ if eq .chezmoi.os "darwin" }}
.config/systemd
{{ end }}
{{ if eq .chezmoi.os "linux" }}
Library
{{ end }}

## The pinentry tooling runs on Linux only. There the wrapper is the
## pinentry-program of gpg-agent and the switcher manages that line.
## On every other host the tooling has no consumer, so it stays in the
## tree for reference and chezmoi does not copy it to $HOME.
{{ if ne .chezmoi.os "linux" }}
.shell/helper/pinentry-switch
.shell/helper/pinentry-wrapper
.shell/zsh/completions/_pinentry-switch
{{ end }}

